{
  "coreName": "DuckStation",
  "systemNames": {
    "PS1": "PlayStation",
    "NAMCOS11": "Namco System 11",
    "KONAMIGQ": "Konami GQ"
  },
  "author": "Connor McLaughlin (stenzek) and the DuckStation contributors",
  "url": "https://github.com/stenzek/duckstation",
  "romFile": "disc",
  "deterministic": true,
  "memoryLayoutMiB": [
    512,
    16,
    1280,
    16,
    4096
  ],
  "_memoryLayoutMiB_note": "sbrk, sealed, invisible, plain, mmap. A PlayStation is small - 2MB of RAM, 1MB of VRAM, a 512KB ROM - and what fills memory is the picture: at 16x the hardware renderer's VRAM is 16384x8192 pixels, 512MB (an arcade board's twice that), and when a real graphics card draws it that much again is kept so a state can carry it (mmap) and once more as the buffer the card writes into (invisible, in no state); the frame handed over is up to 12288x9216, 450MB (mmap). The numbers are room, not use: a savestate holds the pages that were touched. waterbox/run-wbx.c carries the same five and must be kept in step.",
  "video": {
    "_comment": "The frame is the console's own raster: as wide and as tall as the video mode makes it, from 256x224 to 640x480 on an NTSC console and to 576 lines on a PAL one, with the picture where the game put it - times the Internal Resolution when the hardware renderer draws it. The live size is reported per frame; 12288x9216 is room for the widest mode with no overscan cropped, at 16x. It is room, not a buffer Chimera holds: it keeps as much as the picture drawn. It is shown 4:3, as a television showed every one of those modes.",
    "width": 12288,
    "height": 9216,
    "virtualWidth": 640,
    "virtualHeight": 480,
    "vsyncNumerator": 53693175,
    "vsyncDenominator": 897619,
    "_comment_vsync": "An NTSC console's field rate: the video crystal over 3413 ticks a line and 263 lines, 59.826Hz. A PAL console is 53203425 over 3406 by 314, 49.761Hz. The core reports the one the machine booted with (GetVsyncNumerator/Denominator), which the disc's region decides when the Region setting is auto.",
    "getBgra": "GetVideoBgra",
    "gpuStatesSurviveTheContext": true,
    "_comment_gpuStates": "The hardware renderer notices when its GL calls land on a context that is not the one its objects came from (the bridge's context id, which moves on every load), lets go of everything, builds a device again and uploads the console's video memory - which the core fetched from the card before the state was taken. So a state this core makes on a real GPU opens again in a later session.",
    "drawEveryFrame": true,
    "_comment_drawEveryFrame": "Never tell this core to stop drawing; turbo skips only the readback. What the hardware renderer draws stays in video memory on the card and the next frame is drawn over it, so a frame not drawn is a picture lost, not work deferred."
  },
  "audio": {
    "_comment": "The SPU puts out 44100Hz stereo, 737 frames of it per NTSC field and 886 per PAL one. Nothing is stretched or resampled on the way: a frame's samples are the ones the chip produced.",
    "rate": 44100,
    "samplesPerFrame": 2048,
    "channels": 2,
    "get": "GetAudio"
  },
  "lag": {
    "inputWasRead": "InputWasRead"
  },
  "_machines_note": "ONE core.wbx, and two emulators in it. The PlayStation is DuckStation. The arcade boards built out of a PlayStation - the same processor and video chip with a board's own sound hardware, rom board and panel around them - are ArcadeDuck, which began as the same commit of DuckStation and took the console's controllers and memory cards out to put the boards in. Each is linked whole, with its names kept to itself, and the machine setting picks which one a session is (waterbox/machine.h). What they share stays above; what makes them different machines is below. A rom set decides which board it is for - ArcadeDuck's own record says - and a set for another board is refused at load: the machine is the project's, not the file's. Only boards that have run a game here are offered. ArcadeDuck also knows Sony's ZN-1 and ZN-2 and the boards built on them (Capcom, Taito FX-1, Tecmo TPS, Raizing and others) and Konami's GV; they are compiled in and are not declared until a complete set has been booted on each.",
  "machineSetting": "machine",
  "machines": [
    {
      "id": "PS1",
      "label": "PlayStation",
      "when": [
        "playstation"
      ],
      "input": {
        "name": "PlayStation Controllers",
        "_comment": "Eight sockets, and for each every control any controller this console had could put there; then the console's own controls. P1 and P2 are the two ports. P3, P4 and P5 are the three more sockets a multitap in port 1 gives (1B, 1C, 1D) and P6, P7 and P8 are port 2's (2B, 2C, 2D) - the emulator's own order of pads, so a movie made without a multitap and one made with it name port 2 the same way. What a socket holds is its Controller setting, and the core says which of these wires are live: a digital pad is the first fourteen; a DualShock or an analog joystick adds L3, R3, the Analog button and the two sticks; a NeGcon is the d-pad, Start, A, B and R with its Twist and its three analog buttons I, II and L as axes; a GunCon is Trigger, A, B and its aim; a Justifier is Trigger, Start, Back and its aim; the mouse is its two buttons and how far it moved. Shoot Offscreen is the shot fired with the gun pointed away from the screen, which is how those games reload. A stick axis is the byte the pad reports - 0 at the left or the top, 255 at the right or the bottom, 128 at rest - and so is the Twist; I, II and L are 0 when not pressed and 255 pressed home. Gun X and Gun Y are where the gun is aimed, 0..65535 across the picture and down it. Mouse X and Mouse Y are how far the mouse moved this frame, in its own counts. Reset is the button on the case. Previous Disc and Next Disc move a selector over the project's discs and touch nothing; Disc Swap is the lid - up for as long as it is held, and closing on the selected disc (or, with the selector untouched, on the next one).",
